    <comments>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:12pt;color:WindowText;"&gt;J K Rowling's sequel to &lt;span style="font-style:italic;"&gt;Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone&lt;/span&gt; carries on where the original left off. Harry is returning to Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry after the summer holidays and, right from the start, things are not straightforward.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Unable to board the Hogwarts express, Harry and his friends break all the rules and make their way to the school in a magical flying car. From this point on, incredible events happen to Harry and his friends--Harry hears evil voices and someone, or something is attacking the pupils. Can Harry get to the bottom of the mystery before it's too late?&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;As with its predecessor &lt;span style="font-style:italic;"&gt;Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets&lt;/span&gt; is a highly readable and imaginative adventure story with real, fallible, characters, plenty of humour and, of course, loads of magic and spells. There is no need to have read &lt;span style="font-style:italic;"&gt;Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone&lt;/span&gt; to enjoy this book. However, if you have read it, this is the book you have been waiting for... (Ages 9 to Adult)--&lt;span style="font-style:italic;"&gt;Philippa Reece&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
